We can write out the values of these binomial coefficients explicitly. First, we have $\dbinom{7}{3} = \frac{7!}{3!4!} = \frac{7\cdot 6\cdot 5}{3\cdot 2\cdot 1} = 35$. Then, we have $\dbinom{4}{2} = \frac{4!}{2!2!} = \frac{4\cdot 3}{2\cdot 1} = 6$. The total number of ways to do this is therefore $35\cdot 6 = \boxed{210}$.

